  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry with fall-like weather tonight through Sat \nnight. Lows both nights mainly in the 40s-lower 50s. Highs Sat \nmainly in the 60s perhaps flirting with 70 in the lower CT River \nValley.\n\nA very pleasant fall-like airmass is on tap for the region tonight \nthrough Sat night. This as high pressure builds eastward across \nQuebec tonight and into northern New England/Canadian Maritimes Sat \ninto Sat night.\n\nDrier and cooler air works into the region tonight. Winds should \ndecouple enough overnight for lows to drop into the 40s across many \nlocations...but lower-middle 50s in some urban centers as well as \nthe Cape/Islands where a stiff N breeze will keep temps from \nbottoming out. Plenty of sunshine on Sat...but cool NE flow will \nresult in highs mainly in the 60s perhaps flirting with 70 in parts \nof the Lower CT River Valley. Coolest high temps Sat will be found \nalong the immediate eastern MA coast, Cape and Islands where highs \nwill be held in the lower to middle 60s. \n\nHigh pressure slowly shifts east of the region Sat night. \nHowever...think there will be enough of a window for a good night of \nradiational cooling before thicker mid-level clouds move in toward \ndaybreak. Low temps should be well down into the 40s in most \nlocations with lower 50s in the urban heat island of Boston. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Next chance for rain Sunday into Monday. There\nremains uncertainty in the details such as coverage, timing, and \namounts. Showers may linger through early next week. A coastal \nlow pressure is possible late next week.\n\nThe latest guidance still suggests a widespread rainfall later\nSunday into Monday morning. This is not looking like it will be\nan especially dynamic system. Still a wetting rain should\noverspread southern New England from west to east during the\nday. The most likely period for sustained rainfall should be\nSunday night. Then the question becomes how quickly a front\npushes south of our region Monday morning.\n\nRight now, favoring dry weather for most of Monday, continuing\nthrough Wednesday. Some of the deterministic models suggest a\nslightly farther north position of this front, keeping a small\nchance for continuing precipitation towards the south coast.\nGiven the strength of the high pressure over eastern Canada,\nhave gone away from this particular solution.\n\nFinally, the guidance suite is hinting at a possible coastal low\npressure some time Thursday into Friday. However, there are\nrather large variances in track and timing. For example, the 12Z\nGFS has a low pressure over the Gulf of Maine at 8 AM next\nFriday. Meanwhile, the 12Z ECMWF has this same low pressure\nabout 300 miles east of southern MD for this same time.\nDifferent positions also lead to different tracks due to\ninteractions with a mid level trough. At this time, have low\nconfidence in the forecast details late next week. It is a\nsituation that will need to be monitored.",
